{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive 'critical preference' grounded in the logical asymmetry of falsification and the goal of verisimilitude, not probability; accepting this costs us the comfort of believing our chosen theory is likely true, forcing us to act on the best-uncorroborated guess while acknowledging it may still be false.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude cannot be logically defined without invoking inductive assumptions about the future resembling the past.","If the preference for action implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature (that what worked before will work again).","If the distinction between 'critical preference' and 'inductive belief' collapses under the pressure of practical decision-making where outcomes matter."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'justification of belief' (which requires induction and is rejected) and 'rational choice of action' (which relies on the logical status of having survived severe tests). A theory that has survived severe tests has higher informational content and closer proximity to truth (verisimilitude) than a refuted one, making it the only rational candidate for action without asserting its future success is probable.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}